#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130215} :activity/id #uuid "c75874bf-fc95-5847-9b2c-aa230e68a787" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050605 :topic/id #uuid "57e6a947-3892-51bc-87c4-745804dd7be6" :topic/math-academy-id 2056} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130215 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130458 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to evaluate the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1\\int_{x^2}^{1} \\int_{0}^{1}\\,\\dfrac{e^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\textrm{d}z\\, \\textrm{d}y\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFor this repeated integral, the integration domain is the type I region \n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\:  x^2 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nas illustrated below.\n\n![](images/54/54bdc97100c19657d261f35123eec8a4d2d927f0d92073462ae6e9335aef0921.png)\n\nThe expression '$\\,\\textrm{d}z\\, \\textrm{d}y\\, \\textrm{d}x$' means we should integrate first with respect to $z,$ then with respect to $y,$ and lastly with respect to $x.$ Let's see what happens when we attempt to integrate in this order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot [z]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot proceed any further because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of $\\dfrac{e^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}.$\n\nTo resolve this issue, we first note that the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane is the type I plane region $D$ shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f681a48038d51cdb592b265e7f482aa03fffb2d1235df02b38390083fe73f3a.png)\n\nThis region can also be represented as the type II plane region.\n\n![](images/89/89836e63d7f184624334daef7c50239dc818324f24071118aa41a2a1ca5accb6.png)\n\nUsing set notation, $D$'s representation as a type II plane region is\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate this integral by keeping the inner integral the same and swapping the order of integration in the middle and outer integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate this integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}[z]_{0}^{1}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot [x]_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\sqrt{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={e}^{y}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =e-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote the following:\n\n- In this lesson, we'll learn how to compute triple integrals by changing the representation of the projection $D$ of $R$ from a type I plane region to type II (and vice-versa).\n\n- It's also possible to change the spatial (i.e., three-dimensional) representation of $R$ from type I to type II, type I to type III, type II to type III, etc. This is slightly more involved, so we'll leave that discussion for a separate lesson.\n\n$$\n%\\eqalign{\n%\\int_0^1\\int_0^1\\int_{{x}}^{1} \\,6z e^{zy^2}\\,\\textrm{d}y\\, \\textrm{d}x\\, \\, \\textrm{d}z &= \\int_0^1\\int_0^1\\int_{{0}}^{y} \\,6z e^{zy^2}\\,\\textrm{d}x\\, \\textrm{d}y\\, \\, \\textrm{d}z\\\n%&= \\int_0^1\\int_0^1 \\,6z e^{zy^2} \\left[x\\right]_{{0}}^{y}\\, \\textrm{d}y\\, \\, \\textrm{d}z\\\n%&= \\int_0^1\\int_0^1 \\,6z ye^{zy^2} \\, \\textrm{d}y\\, \\, \\textrm{d}z\\\n%&=  \\int_0^1 \\, 3 \\left[e^{zy^2}\\right]_0^1 \\,  \\, \\textrm{d}z\\\n%&=  \\int_0^1 \\, 3 \\left(e^z-1\\right)  \\, \\textrm{d}z\\\n%&= 3e-6.}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0583a558-7d9d-59a9-b3df-1804f157fe28" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7287 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f86e4250-7fc0-50bb-a0af-7ee8be7d9280" :step/math-academy-id 43192 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130221}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130221 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129277 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128855 :question/id #uuid "64c86335-cc36-5b99-a8e3-f2658435b29b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12401"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3337f1e5-3b55-5e56-8003-d80e2a1293c2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050524}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554646}] :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration by Changing the Projection Type"} :step/id #uuid "30b66795-9d4b-59ba-8e94-019dc84aa2a9" :step/math-academy-id 44531 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130223}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130223 :step/content {:db/id 